":"oncologist","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"one who specialises in oncology","word":"oncologista"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"There's been a lot of screening, and people still die,\" says Ian Thompson, a urologic oncologist at the University of Texas Health Science Center, San Antonio.","ref":"2009 February 13, Jennifer Couzin, “BIOMARKERS: Metabolite in Urine May Point To High-Risk Prostate Cancer”, in Science, volume 323, number 5916, →DOI:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[86,96]],"id":"5da6b56a-f1d0-4607-9b83-c8e29d298eef"},{"text":"When considered in combination with the use of antiangiogenic therapy, this diagnostic tool is easy to apply and has broad utility among medical oncologists to avoid the risk of hemosputum in the great vessels [ 5 ].","ref":"2015 July 30, Hidetaka Uramoto et al., “Easy Diagnosis of Aortic Invasion in Patients with Lung Cancer Using Cine Magnetic Resonance Imaging”, in Case Reports in Oncology, volume 8, →DOI:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[145,156]],"id":"6dba10c9-30a1-4abc-8e60-94974cdb5a75"}],"topics":["medicine","oncology","sciences"],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English terms prefixed with onco-","English terms suffixed with -ist","English terms suffixed with -logist","English terms with quotations","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Terms with Asturian translations","Terms with Catalan translations","Terms with Czech translations","Terms with Dutch translations","Terms with Esperanto translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with Galician translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Greek translations","Terms with Icelandic translations","Terms with Indonesian translations","Terms with Irish translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Mandarin translations","Terms with Māori translations","Terms with Occitan translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Romanian translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Swedish translations","Terms with Turkish translations","en:Biologists","en:Healthcare occupations","en:Oncology"],"links":[["oncology","oncology"],["doctor","doctor"],["scientist","scientist"],["oncology","oncology"]],"raw_glosses":["(oncology) A doctor or scientist who specializes in oncology."],"glosses":["A doctor or scientist who specializes in oncology."],"id":"3e86bfa0-f8ce-4a11-b0fc-54d41f1d4500"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"once in a blue moon":{"id":"827d7094-8edf-458a-ada1-9c3c1313c779","word":"once in a blue moon","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"very rarely","word":"de vez em nunca"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"very rarely","word":"uma vez na vida, outra na morte"}],"pos":"adv","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"We are no advocates for the eternal system of producing foreign operas to the exclusion of the works of English composers, but once in a blue moon such a thing may be allowed.","ref":"1833, J. Lection (publisher), The Athenæum, page 780:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[127,146]],"id":"bd110c4e-f82a-4fc7-a454-8cf80898bbda"},{"text":"[T]ime expanded and space contracted, and the name Blue Moon took on a symbolic meaning, as if the future, so delicately plausible, were of a kind that might happen once in a blue moon only.","ref":"1933, James Hilton, Lost Horizon, published 1973, page 133:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[165,184]],"id":"a2a57048-371c-41b6-9368-dc2a4dad84e8"}],"categories":["Cantonese terms with redundant transliterations","English adverbs","English entries with incorrect language header","English idioms","English lemmas","English multiword terms","English positive pol